`dafny verify` now verifies a constructor that a refining module inherits unchanged when the module adds a field that the constructor must assign (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/411). A constructor must assign every field that is subject to definite-assignment rules before `new;`, or by the end of its body. The copied constructor checks that, but `dafny verify` never verified members that a refining module inherits unchanged, so it never reported the field that the inherited constructor cannot assign, and a read of that field could prove `false`. The compile commands, which verify the whole program, already reported it. The error is reported at the inherited constructor, with the refining module as a related location, also when the base module comes from an included file or a library. Only the field checks are new: the copied body's other assertions are still assumed, as the base module proved them. To initialize the new field, the refining module declares the constructor itself. The hidden option `--legacy-inherited-constructor-fields` restores the old, unsound rule, for migration and comparison only.
